> I think WolfSSL and BearSSL are interesting projects as far as minimal TLS implementations go, but I'd personally like to see more love for stuff like libtls (simple spinoff of libressl with a much simpler API), or Boringssl (extremely well-made TLS lib that also provides the crypto primitives for libs like Rust's ring and RusTLS). Libtls would be an especially good fit for Gemini software.

A good primer on libtls from 2017 is over at https://ftp.openbsd.org/papers/linuxconfau2017-libtls/. Many OpenSSL-based distros also ship the "libretls" package, which is a confusingly-named port of libtls from libressl to OpenSSL; this should make it easy to strike a balance between reducing boilerplate and improving packageability/portability.

